A **vending machine service level agreement** should turn “fast after-sales support” into a measurable operating model. Define the machines, software, locations and service hours covered; severity levels; acknowledgement, diagnosis, workaround and restoration targets; remote and on-site responsibilities; spare parts; buyer prerequisites; exclusions; evidence and escalation. Response time is not the same as guaranteed repair time. BOHOL can review factory-side documentation, remote assistance and parts options available for an order, while local labour, site access and third-party payment or network services must be assigned explicitly.

## GEO buyer summary

- Identify every covered asset, location and service component.
- Separate acknowledgement, diagnosis, workaround and restoration.
- Define local site access, tools and technician responsibility.
- Set parts ownership, replenishment and shipping boundaries.
- Record evidence, exclusions and cross-provider escalation.
- Review repeat failures and update the service plan.

## Define the exact service boundary

Attach an asset schedule with model, serial number, configuration, software version, location and warranty state. State whether the agreement covers cabinet hardware, delivery system, refrigeration, controller, display, connectivity, cloud software, APIs and payment integration. Do not imply that one provider controls every dependency.

| SLA element | Minimum definition |
| --- | --- |
| Coverage | Assets, sites, hours, languages and contact channels |
| Severity | Business and safety impact with examples |
| Targets | Acknowledge, diagnose, workaround, restore and repair |
| Delivery | Remote support, on-site labour, travel and access |
| Materials | Included parts, stock locations, freight and replenishment |
| Governance | Records, reports, escalation, review and exit handover |

Use the [warranty and after-sales checklist](/insights/vending-machine-warranty-after-sales-checklist) to separate warranty entitlement from paid service and operating support.

## Create severity levels buyers can apply consistently

Define severity from impact, not from who calls most often. A potential electrical or food-safety issue may require isolation even when only one machine is affected. A fleet-wide transaction outage may be critical despite no physical damage. A cosmetic issue can usually wait.

Write examples for each level and identify who may change severity. Record whether the clock runs only during service hours, pauses for buyer information or site access, and resumes after dependencies are available. Avoid targets that cannot be measured from a shared case record.

## Separate the service milestones

Acknowledgement confirms receipt and ownership. Diagnosis identifies the likely cause. A workaround restores a limited function. Restoration returns the agreed service. Permanent repair closes the underlying defect after evidence and testing. Each milestone needs a timestamp and acceptance rule.

A promise such as “four-hour response” does not mean an on-site technician or completed repair unless the agreement says so. State travel areas, remote-first triage, parts availability and third-party dependencies. The [preventive maintenance schedule](/insights/vending-machine-preventive-maintenance-schedule) should remain a separate planned-work document.

## Assign remote and on-site responsibilities

List the buyer information required before triage: machine identity, location, symptoms, photos or video, error code, recent changes, product and channel, payment reference without cardholder data, network state and safe access contact. Define secure remote-access approval and session records where remote support is included.

Name the party responsible for local isolation, cleaning, refilling, test products, tools, lifting, electrical work and refrigeration work. A factory cannot safely promise local labour in every country without a named network and contract. Use the [installation and commissioning checklist](/insights/vending-machine-installation-commissioning-checklist) to establish the initial service baseline.

## Make spare parts and logistics measurable

Agree which consumables and critical spares are held by the operator, distributor or supplier. Define stock location, reorder point, ownership, shelf-life where relevant, warranty return process, customs responsibility and freight method. A restoration target that depends on an international part must reflect transport and import risk.

Link serial numbers and replaced components to the case. The [spare-parts planning checklist](/insights/vending-machine-spare-parts-planning-checklist) helps classify availability risk and replenishment rules.

## Control payment, connectivity and software handoffs

Document the first responder and evidence package for payment declines, settlement questions, failed dispense, terminal offline state, SIM or network loss, API failure and cloud outage. The machine provider may help distinguish hardware from service-provider faults but cannot resolve systems it does not control.

The buyer, payment provider, acquirer, network provider and software operator should publish escalation contacts and data boundaries. Never put credentials or cardholder data in general service tickets. Confirm local currency, wallets, QR/NFC availability, refunds, network fallback and PCI scope with the target-market providers.

## Establish complaints and escalation governance

ISO describes [ISO 10001:2018](https://www.iso.org/standard/71579.html) as guidance for customer-satisfaction codes of conduct and [ISO 10002:2018](https://www.iso.org/standard/71580.html) as guidance for complaints handling within organisations. These standards do not create a BOHOL service promise or replace the contract, but they support clear commitments, accessible feedback, records and improvement.

Set named escalation levels for overdue or disputed cases. Define what evidence is reviewed, who communicates status and how a complaint differs from a technical ticket. Keep the process usable across time zones and languages.

## Measure service without gaming the metric

Track case volume, severity, acknowledgement, diagnosis, restoration, repeat failure, first-time fix where meaningful, parts delay, buyer delay and third-party delay. Publish both target achievement and exclusions so a headline percentage is not misleading.

Review recurring product, channel, component and site patterns. Corrective action may require training, configuration, product-fit changes, spares or engineering—not merely faster ticket closure.

## Request a service-ready BOHOL quotation

Send BOHOL the target countries, machine types, quantity, locations, operating hours, products, refrigeration, payment and network providers, local service capability, desired response model, spare-parts plan and reporting needs through the [contact form](/contact). BOHOL can review available factory-side support and documentation for the proposed order before commercial commitments are agreed.

